VIEWS_BODY = <<-'TXT'
  <p><code>render "posts/index"</code> draws this file inside <code>layouts/application.eiy</code>. A typo here is a build error, not a 500. The view reads the controller's instance variables as they are.</p><ul><li><strong>Escaping.</strong> <code>&lt;%= %&gt;</code> escapes; <code>raw(x)</code> does not.</li><li><strong>Partials.</strong> A name ending in <code>.eiy</code>: <code>render "_form.eiy", post: @post</code>, or once per item with <code>collection:</code>.</li><li><strong>Layouts.</strong> <code>layout: "admin"</code> picks another; <code>layout: false</code> draws none.</li></ul><table><thead><tr><th>Helper</th><th>Prints</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>link_to "Edit", path</code></td><td>a link</td></tr><tr><td><code>button_to "Delete", path, method: :delete</code></td><td>a form with its CSRF field</td></tr><tr><td><code>pluralize(3, "comment")</code></td><td>3 comments</td></tr><tr><td><code>time_ago_in_words(t)</code></td><td>about 2 hours</td></tr><tr><td><code>simple_format(text)</code></td><td>paragraphs and line breaks</td></tr></tbody></table>
  TXT

# controllers
CONTROLLERS_CODE = <<-'TXT'
  class PostsController < ApplicationController
    before_action :set_post, only: [:show, :edit, :update, :destroy]

    # GET /posts
    def index
      @posts = Post.order_desc(:id).paginate(params[:page]?, per: 25).all!
      render "posts/index"
    end

    # GET /posts/:id
    def show
      render "posts/show"
    end

    # GET /posts/new
    def new
      render "posts/new"
    end

    # POST /posts
    def create
      @post = Post.new(post_params)
      if @post.save!
        redirect_to path_to(@post), notice: "Post was created."
      else
        render "posts/new", status: 422
      end
    end

    # GET /posts/:id/edit
    def edit
      render "posts/edit"
    end

    # PATCH /posts/:id
    def update
      if @post.update(post_params)!
        redirect_to path_to(@post), notice: "Post was updated."
      else
        render "posts/edit", status: 422
      end
    end

    # DELETE /posts/:id
    def destroy
      @post.destroy!
      redirect_to "/posts", notice: "Post was deleted."
    end

    # The record the action works on; a missing one is a 404 before the
    # action runs.
    private def set_post
      @post = Post.find(params[:id])!
    end

    # The fields the form may set: strong parameters.
    private def post_params : Hash(String, String)
      params.require(:post).permit(:title, :body, :published)
    end
  end
  TXT
CONTROLLERS_BODY = <<-'TXT'
  <ul><li><strong>Each public method is an action.</strong> A route reaches it by name.</li><li><strong>Filters run first.</strong> <code>before_action</code>, with <code>only:</code> or <code>except:</code>. One that answers <code>Unauthorized</code>, <code>Forbidden</code>, <code>NotFound</code> or <code>DBError</code> stops the action.</li><li><strong>Params are text.</strong> <code>params[:id]</code> is <code>""</code> when missing, <code>params[:q]?</code> is nil. <code>permit</code> is the only way from a form to a model, and converts to the field types.</li><li><strong><code>@post</code> and <code>@posts</code> need no declaration.</strong> A variable named after a model holds that model, its plural an array of it, so neither is ever nil.</li></ul>
  TXT

# errors
ERRORS_CODE = <<-'TXT'
  class ApplicationController < Controller
    before_action :require_login, except: [:index, :show]

    # A filter that answers a Halt stops the action.
    def require_login
      Unauthorized.new unless session["user_id"]?
    end
  end
  TXT
ERRORS_BODY = <<-'TXT'
  <table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NotFound</code></td><td>404</td></tr><tr><td><code>Invalid</code></td><td>422, with the reasons</td></tr><tr><td><code>Unauthorized</code></td><td>a redirect to <code>/login</code>; 401 for JSON</td></tr><tr><td><code>Forbidden</code></td><td>403</td></tr><tr><td><code>DBError</code></td><td>503; the details only in the log</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An application's own page for any of them is registered before <code>install_error_pages</code>: <code>router.error NotFound do |env, err| ... end</code>.</p>
  TXT

# routes
ROUTES_CODE = <<-'TXT'
  # Every route of the application.
  #
  #   resources "posts", PostsController       the REST routes of a resource
  #   get "/about", PagesController, :about    one route (post, put, patch, delete too)
  #   resources "posts", PostsController do    nested: /posts/:post_id/comments
  #     resources "comments", CommentsController
  #   end
  #   namespace "admin" do ... end             routes under /admin
  #
  # `kurt routes` lists them all.

  root HomeController, :index
  resources "posts", PostsController
  TXT
ROUTES_OUTPUT = <<-'TXT'
    Verb    Path               Controller#Action
    GET     /                  HomeController#index
    GET     /posts             PostsController#index
    GET     /posts/new         PostsController#new
    POST    /posts             PostsController#create
    GET     /posts/:id         PostsController#show
    GET     /posts/:id/edit    PostsController#edit
    PATCH   /posts/:id         PostsController#update
    PUT     /posts/:id         PostsController#update
    POST    /posts/:id         PostsController#update
    DELETE  /posts/:id         PostsController#destroy
    POST    /posts/:id/delete  PostsController#destroy
    GET     /up                health check
  TXT
ROUTES_BODY = <<-'TXT'
  <ul><li><strong><code>resources</code> gives the REST routes</strong> for exactly the actions the controller defines. <code>only:</code> and <code>except:</code> register fewer.</li><li><strong>A block nests resources.</strong> <code>/posts/:post_id/comments</code> reaches a controller that finds its parent with <code>params[:post_id]</code>.</li><li><strong><code>namespace "admin"</code> prefixes paths.</strong> </li><li><strong>HTML forms send PATCH and DELETE</strong> through <code>_method</code>; that is why the table also has <code>POST</code> rows.</li></ul>
  TXT

# tests
TESTS_CODE = <<-'TXT'
  # `kurt test` runs this against db/test.sqlite3, freshly migrated, through
  # the routes and without a socket.
  Post.delete_all.as(Int64)
  app = RouteHandler.new(routes)

  res = dispatch(app, "GET", "/posts").response
  assert res.status_code == 200

  # a blank title is refused, and the form comes back
  res = dispatch(app, form_request("POST", "/posts", "post%5Btitle%5D=")).response
  assert res.status_code == 422
  assert Post.count.as(Int64) == 0

  # a valid form creates one and redirects to it
  res = dispatch(app, form_request("POST", "/posts", "post%5Btitle%5D=Example&post%5Bbody%5D=Example&post%5Bpublished%5D=1")).response
  assert res.status_code == 303
  post = Post.last?.as(Post)
  assert res.headers["Location"]? == "/posts/" + post.id.to_s
  assert dispatch(app, "GET", "/posts/" + post.id.to_s).response.status_code == 200

  # a missing one is 404
  assert dispatch(app, "GET", "/posts/999999").response.status_code == 404

  # deleted through the form-friendly route
  res = dispatch(app, form_request("POST", "/posts/" + post.id.to_s + "/delete", "")).response
  assert res.status_code == 303
  assert Post.count.as(Int64) == 0
  TXT
TESTS_OUTPUT = <<-'TXT'
  1 passed, 0 failed
  TXT
TESTS_BODY = <<-'TXT'
  <p><code>kurt test</code> migrates a fresh test database and runs every <code>test/*_test.iyi</code>, each starting from the rows in <code>test/fixtures/*.yml</code>. A label's id is the same on every run, so fixtures name each other:</p><pre><code># test/fixtures/posts.yml
  welcome:
    title: Welcome
    user: ada          # the id of ada in users.yml</code></pre><p>A test reads a row as <code>posts(:welcome)</code>, and <code>sign_in_as(users(:ada))</code> signs the next requests in.</p>
  TXT

# dev
